    "reasoning_content": "Excellent findings. Key sources so far:\n\n1. Capsida corporate deck PDF (open): https://capsida.com/wp-content/uploads/Nick_Goeden_1445_May-14_Theater-C_Identification-of-Multiple-BBB-Receptors.pdf — has NR001-NR005, plate-based and bead-based receptor binding detection, NHP sequence identity, \"predictive power of in vitro receptor binding is confirmed by AAV transduction\", \"Binding also confirmed for NR002-5\", \"Human and NHP ortholog binding confirmed\", \"NR004: endothelial-specific\", \"NR002: broad expression\". Great quotes.\n\n2. Lir Starfish blog: https://www.lirtx.com/post/starfish-fast-capsid-structure-generation — quotes about3-fold axis, RMSD <1Å, Boltz2 OOM on H100 80GB at 5-fold, AF2/Openfold >1 hour for 5-fold axis, docking LY6A, \"screening tens of thousands of variants\", \"monomer only no detectable signal\", \"AAV has a relatively simple capsid made up for 60 subunits\". Great.\n\n3. Zhang et al. 2019 Nature Microbiology \"AAV2 bound to AAVR\": PKD2 binds spike region adjacent to icosahedral three-fold axis; PDB 6IHB. Nature Microbiology may be paywalled? The abstract page is open (nature.com abstracts are open). PMC version exists: https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6561701/ (Meyer et al. 2019 eLife - open). Also PMC9950413 review (open) with quotes about PKD2 near 3-fold axis.\n\n4. ALPL paper (Molecular Therapy 2025 / biorxiv preprint 2024.03.12.584703 open): ALPL as receptor for VCAP-102, KD ~20 nM, AlphaFold2-multimer predicted structure of ALPL dimer + VCAP-102 VP3 trimer, in silico modeling predicts peptide binds ALPL catalytic pocket. The Voyager ASGCT poster PDF is open too.\n\nNow I need:\n- LRP6 in AAV/BBB context. Let me search. Possibly LRP6 is a Wnt co-receptor at BBB; maybe there's a paper on AAV binding LRP6? If not, DESIGN: LRP6 included as panel partner. Actually the option says \"the human BBB-receptor set used by Capsida's NR001–NR005 platform and Lir's Starfish capsid-structure pipeline (ALPL, AAVR, LRP6 and their panel partners)\". Hmm, LRP6 might be one of Capsida's NRs? Unknown. I'll search briefly.\n\n- AlphaFold-Multimer / ColabFold runtime on GPU (for PROXY numbers).\n- GROMACS/OpenMM MD throughput (ns/day) on A100/H100 (PROXY).\n- Docking tool open source (LightDock or HADDOCK) and its throughput.\n- AAV capsid T=1 60 subunits (have it from Lir blog and the coarse-grained paper).\n- Number of published AAV–receptor complexes (for baseline \"single-digit complexes\" claim from the option; I should find a citation for \"published modeling covers only single-digit complexes\" — e.g., the review PMC9950413 lists several cryo-EM structures: AAV1, AAV2, AAV9 with PKD2, AAV5 with PKD1, AAVGo.1 — that's ~5-6 complexes → single-digit. Good.\n\n- Binding assay formats: plate-based / bead-based — Capsida deck has the labels. For the proxy binding panel, I could use published bind/no-bind data: e.g., AAV serotype binding to AAVR PKD domains (Pillay 2017?), AAV9-galactose, AAV2-HSPG. Also Retrogenix cell microarray (from ALPL paper) is a surface-protein binding screen — open quote.\n\n- LY6A receptor docking to variant capsid surfaces (Lir ESGCT poster) — good for \"closest prior\": Lir's Starfish + docking screen of LY6A against variant capsid surfaces. But is that \"published\"? A blog post. The novelty.closest_prior should be published result nearest to deciding if_lever vs if_not: likely the Zhang2019/Meyer 2019 structural work (single-digit complexes) or Lir's Starfish blog (docking screens at scale). The prompt says novelty.closest_prior is \"the published result that comes nearest to deciding between if_lever and if_not: what it measured and the largest amount of the compute quantity it used\". Our compute axis is number of capsid×receptor pairs evaluated (or pose jobs). Prior max level: e.g., Lir screened \"tens of thousands of variants\" in silico with Starfish+docking (blog), or published structural studies cover6 complexes (prior_max_level=6 pairs). I'll say prior is the published cryo-EM AAV–AAVR complexes + the ALPL/VCAP-102 AF2-multimer modeling, max level =6 complexes on the same axis (capsid–receptor pairs with binding readout + geometry). decides: no.\n\nNow the compute axis. Let me think about what \"more parallel compute buys\": the option says \"More parallel compute buys model runs across capsid × receptor × pose for the NR001–NR005 receptor set, letting the shared-motif question be tested receptor by receptor\". So compute_axis.quantity = \"capsid–receptor pairs each run through pose enumeration, evaluated against binding readouts\" or \"pose jobs\". Levels: e.g., 12, 48, 120 pairs (capsids × receptors). Let me design the grid:\n\nCapsids (public stand-ins for NR-associated capsids): 8 capsids: AAV2, AAV5, AAV8, AAV9, AAVrh10, AAV-DJ, VCAP-102 (from preprint — sequence? VCAP-102 is an AAV9 variant; sequence may not be public. Hmm. If sequence not public, use AAV9 as stand-in and cite). Also AAV1. Let me pick 6 capsids with public structures: AAV1 (PDB 3NG9?), AAV2 (1LP3/6IH9), AAV5 (PDB?), AAV8, AAV9 (PDBs), AAVrh10. That's 6.\n\nReceptors: ALPL, AAVR (PKD12 or PKD2), LRP6 (ectodomain β-propeller), + panel partners. Panel partners per ALPL paper: Ly6A, Ly6C1, CA4 (GPI-AP family BBB receptors) — citable from Molecular Therapy quote \"several other receptors used by BBB-penetrant AAV capsids, namely Ly6A, Ly6C1, and CA4, belong to the GPI-AP family\". Also AAVR as control. And maybe TfR (transferrin receptor) as comparator. So receptor panel: ALPL, AAVR-PKD2, LRP6, Ly6A, CA4, TfR = 6 receptors. Grid6 capsids × 6 receptors = 36 pairs. Pose jobs: 3 pose generators × N seeds: e.g., 24 poses/pair × 36 = 864 pose evaluations; with3 seeds for AF-Multimer: 36×3=108 AF-Multimer runs; docking 36×1 (each returning 100 poses); MD refine top 3 poses per pair = 108 MD runs of 20 ns each.\n\nCompute axis levels: number of capsid–receptor pairs: 12 (pilot: 3 capsids × 4 receptors), 36 (full grid), 72 (full grid × 2 pose families/seeds? or 2× more poses). Hmm — \"the quantity this test varies that more parallel compute buys\" = pairs (or pair×pose runs). The \"answer needs\" N runs. With restructuring method none, compute_axis = \"the number of runs the answer needs\": e.g., \"capsid–receptor pair evaluations\" with levels [12, 36, 72]. Or pose jobs with levels [288, 864, 1728].\n\nI like: quantity = \"capsid–receptor pose runs scored against binding readouts\", unit = \"pose runs\", levels = [288, 864, 1728]. Where 864 = 36 pairs × 24 poses. Hmm, but the measured quantity in predictions should be agreement with readouts, which is per-pair. Let me make compute axis = \"capsid–receptor pairs evaluated end-to-end (each with pose enumeration)\" — unit \"pairs\", levels [12, 36, 72]. 72 = 6 capsids × 6 receptors × 2 replicate pose-ensembles... not clean. Alternative: capsids: 6; receptors: 4 core (ALPL, AAVR, LRP6, Ly6A) +2 panel; levels by number of pairs run: 12, 36, 60. Hmm.\n\nCleaner: compute axis quantity = \"independent docking/AF2 pose jobs\", unit = \"jobs\", levels = [216, 864, 1728]. Each job = one capsid–receptor–pose-seed model run. 36 pairs × 6 pose seeds = 216 (pilot-scale), ×24 = 864 (main), ×48 = 1728 (stretch). The measured outcome per level: recall of known binding-positive pairs at ≥ some geometry agreement. Good: \"the number of runs the answer needs\" = 864.\n\nPredictions:\n- Measured quantity: agreement between predicted3-fold engagement + bind calls and published bind/no-bind readouts (balanced accuracy across receptor panel), measured at each compute level (subsampling poses).\n- if_lever: balanced accuracy rises with pose jobs: e.g., ≥0.75 at 864 jobs vs ≤0.55 at 216 jobs; and all known positive pairs (e.g., AAV2–AAVR, AAV9–AAVR, VCAP-102–ALPL proxy, AAV9–Ly6A? (AAV9 binds Ly6a in mouse? Actually Ly6A is the receptor for AAV-PHP.eB in mouse) recovered with 3-fold/known geometry within 5 Å interface RMSD.\n- if_not: accuracy stays within noise (≤0.05 change) from 216 to 864 jobs; more poses don't recover additional positives; agreement stuck near chance/baseline.\n- decision_rule: if balanced accuracy improves by ≥0.10 from 216→864 jobs and at least 3 of 4 known positive pairs are recovered at interface RMSD ≤5 Å to published geometry, call if_lever; else if_not.\n- noise_rule: 3 random seed sets per level; paired bootstrap over the panel; a difference must exceed 2× the seed-to-seed SD of balanced accuracy (or a paired permutation p<0.05 on per-pair calls).\n- expected: \"if_not\" or \"unsure\"? What do I expect? Honestly: more poses probably saturates quickly; the real question is whether 3-fold geometry agreement tracks binding. I expect the compute lever does NOT change the answer much beyond a modest pose count (saturation), i.e., if_not... but the option's premise is that more compute tests receptor-by-receptor. Hmm. The honest expectation: the number of pairs (receptor coverage) is what more compute buys, and the answer (which pairs are binding-positive and whether their geometry is 3-fold) is more likely to be informative with more receptors; but the *agreement* metric may not improve with poses alone. I'd say expected = \"unsure\" is weak; the schema allows \"if_lever\",\"if_not\",\"unsure\". Let me think: the measured quantity = number of binding-positive pairs whose top pose engages the3-fold axis consistent with published geometry, out of known positives (recovery). If we spend compute on more pairs rather than more poses per pair, then if_lever is about coverage. I'll frame compute axis as \"capsid–receptor pair-seed model runs\" and the outcome as \"recovery of known bind/no-bind calls with3-fold-consistent geometry\". Expected: \"if_lever\" for pair coverage up to 36 pairs? Hmm.\n\nLet me define the measured quantity crisply: `recovery_rate` = fraction of the 6 published binding-positive capsid–receptor pairs (AAV2–AAVR, AAV1–AAVR, AAV9–AAVR, AAV5–AAVR-PKD1, VCAP-102–ALPL (AAV9 proxy), AAV-PHP.eB–Ly6A? (mouse Ly6A; human LY6A1? hmm)) whose best-scoring pose both (a) reproduces the published interface (interface RMSD ≤ 5 Å or ≥50% contact overlap) and (b) is called bind. And `false_positive_rate` on negatives.\n\nif_lever: with 864 pose runs vs 216, recovery_rate ≥ 0.83 (5/6) vs ≤0.50, FPR ≤0.2.\nif_not: recovery stays ≤0.5 at all levels (or FPR stays high); more poses merely duplicate the same wrong pose.\n\ndecision rule: measured on the6-pair reference panel, paired across levels.\n\nnoise_rule: 3 seed sets; Wilson CI; require non-overlapping CIs or paired McNemar p<0.05.\n\nexpected: \"if_not\" — why? Because published depth is single-digit complexes and receptor ectodomains are flexible; more poses mainly re-sample the same wrong basin; the binding readouts also vary (assay conditions). Hmm, but the plan's purpose is to test exactly that. I think honest expectation is \"unsure\"? The schema requires expected among the three. I'd say \"if_not\" with the reason that docking/AF2 pose quality saturates quickly and the blocker is receptor conformational ensemble, not pose count. Actually wait — if compute axis is *pairs* (receptor coverage), then more compute = more receptors tested, which does change the answer (more pairs known). But the predictions should be on \"the same measured quantity\". Let me use: \"number of capsid–receptor pairs whose calls match published bind/no-bind and whose top pose matches published interface geometry (out of the reference panel, at each compute level)\". Hmm, recovery out of a fixed panel of 6 doesn't grow with pairs.\n\nBetter: the measured quantity should be per-level: `balanced_accuracy` of the bind/no-bind calls across the reference panel (fixed), evaluated with the pose budget of that level. And separately, more pairs beyond the reference panel are \"yields\" (candidates discovered). So compute axis = pose runs per pair (search depth) × number of pairs (coverage). The option's compute_open says more compute buys runs across capsid×receptor×pose — both coverage and depth.\n\nSimplest coherent choice: compute_axis.quantity = \"capsid–receptor–pose model runs\", unit = \"runs\", levels = [216, 864, 1728]. The reference panel balanced accuracy is measured at each level (subsample runs per pair for lower levels; add pairs/seeds for higher). if_lever: balanced accuracy ≥0.75 at 864 runs and ≥0.10 above 216-run level. if_not: difference ≤0.05.\n\nOK. And \"restructuring\": sequential_part = \"none: each pose job is independent; one answer does not wait on a previous step\". Actually there IS a 3-stage pipeline per pair (structure prep → pose generation → MD refine → score). But the prompt for restructuring: \"sequential_part is the part of this computation that one answer waits on step after step, and how many steps; method is why those steps need not run in order and how your plan computes them at the same time instead, or 'none' if the plan only runs independent work side by side or splits each step across GPUs.\"\n\nHonest answer: per-pair there are 3 sequential stages (pose gen → MD relax → score), 3 steps; but different pairs and poses don't wait on each other. The method: run all pose jobs concurrently; within each job, the 3 stages run as a streaming pipeline across jobs so no GPU idles. Since \"the plan only runs independent work side by side\", method = \"none\" is allowed and simplest. But then the instruction \"test_summary names both baselines\" and \"predictions say where on the axis the parallel version reaches the same answer sooner than each baseline\" — those apply anyway? The paragraph \"Then compute_axis is...\" seems to follow the \"If method is not none\" block but describes general record content. To be safe, I'll set method = \"none\" (independent jobs side by side) BUT still provide baseline_sequential and baseline_copies fields (allowed since they're just optional properties) and name both baselines in test_summary. Hmm, but if method is \"none\", is it weird to give baseline_sequential? The schema allows extra fields within restructuring object (additionalProperties not restricted — properties listed, required only sequential_part and method). Fine, I'll include them; it strengthens the record.\n\nActually wait. Let me reconsider: maybe there IS a genuinely parallelizable chain worth claiming: the per-pair pipeline (docking → AF2 → MD → score) is 4 steps, and the \"method\" is that poses within a pair and across pairs are batched so that the chain is pipelined rather than serialized. The payoff condition: pose jobs have similar runtimes (low variance) so the pipeline stays full; cheap measurement: pilot measures per-job runtime CV. That's a legitimate restructuring claim and gives a real \"if_lever: parallel reaches same answer sooner than baseline_sequential\". The instructions say if method is not none, I must give condition, condition_phase, baseline_sequential, baseline_copies. And then compute_axis \"is the length of the chain or the number of runs the answer needs\". And anchor \"checks the parallel result against the sequential one within a stated tolerance\" — for independent jobs, parallel and sequential give identical results per job (modulo nondeterminism in MD/AF2 seeds). So anchor: run a12-pair subset both sequentially and in parallel; per-pair scores must match within tolerance (e.g., interface RMSD ≤1 Å, score Δ ≤ 0.5 kJ/mol or rank correlationρ≥0.9).\n\nI'll go with method = \"none\" since the option says \"sequential: none: each capsid–receptor pose is an independent docking or AlphaFold-Multimer job with its own seeds\". That matches the option record's own view. And the instruction \"or 'none' if the plan only runs independent work side by side or splits each step across GPUs\" fits. I'll still give baseline_sequential and baseline_copies as informative fields. Hmm, but \"test_summary names both baselines\" — I'll do that regardless. And \"anchor checks the parallel result against the sequential one within a stated tolerance\" — the anchor object can describe this: run the 12-pair pilot subset serially on one GPU vs in parallel on 8; require per-pair top-pose interface RMSD ≤2 Å and identical bind/no-bind calls.\n\nNow cost design. Budget: $1,000. Rates to use:\n- H100 8x: $3.99/GPU/hr → instance $31.92/hr. Good for AF2-multimer big complexes and MD.\n- A100 8x 40GB: $1.99/GPU/hr → $15.92/hr. Cheaper; enough for most jobs (ColabFold on40GB works for ~1000-residue complexes at moderate MSA).\n- A6000 4x: $1.09/GPU/hr → $4.36/hr. For docking + short MD? OpenMM on A6000 is OK.\n\nPlan lines (time boxes):\n1. Phase P0 setup + anchor: 1 GPU (A100 1x $1.99/hr) for 6 hours: install ColabFold/OpenMM/LightDock, download structures (6 capsid structures, receptor structures), reproduce AAV2–AAVR published interface (anchor). Cost 6×1.99 = $11.94. Or use 2-GPU A100 @1.99. Keep simple.\n\n2. Phase P1 pilot measurement: 8x A100 for 3 hours = 24 GPU-hr × $1.99 = $47.76. Measures: AF2-multimer wall-clock per run, MD ns/hour, docking poses/hour, p_3fold geometry, and reference-panel balanced accuracy at pilot depth. Gate: if per-run AF2 time ≤ X hours → proceed at main scale; else shrink grid.\n\n3. Phase P2 main enumeration: 8x H100 for 20 hours = 160 GPU-hr × $3.99 = $638.40. Hmm that's the bulk. Let's compute: 864 pose runs. If AF2-multimer run takes ~0.5 h on one GPU → 864×0.5 = 432 GPU-hr. Too much. Need to be realistic: Not all 864 runs are AF2. Mix: docking (CPU, cheap) generates 24 poses/pair; AF2-multimer only on a subset (e.g., 3 seeds × 36 pairs = 108 runs); MD refine top-3 poses × 36 pairs = 108 MD runs × 20 ns.\n\nLet me restructure the job accounting:\n- Pose generation by rigid-body docking (LightDock/pyDock): CPU-ish; run on the CPU of the instance (208 vCPUs on 8x). 36 pairs × 1 pose job = 36 jobs, each producing 200 poses. Wall-clock maybe 0.5-2 h/pair; run36 in parallel on CPUs. Essentially free GPU-wise (occurs during GPU hours anyway).\n- AF2-multimer/ColabFold: 36 pairs × 3 seeds = 108 runs. Say each run 0.25–0.75 h on one A100 40GB for ~1000 residues (PROXY from ColabFold benchmarks; measure in pilot). 108 × 0.5 h = 54 GPU-hr.\n- MD refinement: top 3 poses/pair × 36 = 108 short MD runs (20 ns each). At 200 ns/day on A100 for a ~100k-atom system (PROXY from GROMACS benchmarks) → 20 ns = 2.4 h each → 108×2.4 = 259 GPU-hr. Too much. Reduce to 10 ns or top-1 pose/pair with36 runs: 36 × 2.4 = 86 GPU-hr. Or use implicit solvent/short 5 ns relax: 0.6 h each × 108 = 65 GPU-hr.\n\nTotal GPU-hr ≈ 54 (AF2) + 65 (MD) + ~20 (overhead/docking) ≈ 140 GPU-hr. On 8×A100 at $1.99/GPU-h = $278.6 wall-clock ~18 h on the 8x instance. Plus pilot and setup. Total well under $1,000. Then we can afford more depth (stretch): 2× pose seeds (1728 runs) or more receptors/capsids.\n\nBut careful: numbers must have basis. AF2 wall-clock per run: I should find a citable source. ColabFold paper: \"reduces AF2 prediction time to ~minutes on a single GPU\"? Let me fetch the ColabFold paper (Nature Methods 2022, open?) or the bioRxiv. Also GROMACS performance: \"GROMACS 2020 paper reports ~ 0.5 μs/day\"? Let me search for citable benchmarks.\n\nAlso useful: OpenMM benchmark page (open) or \"How fast is MD on GPU\" pages. Let me search.\n\nAlso for AF3/Boltz: Lir blog says Boltz2 OOM on H100 80GB for 5-fold axis, AF2/Openfold \"requiring over an hour to generate the 5-fold axis\". That's a citable runtime proxy: \"over an hour to generate the 5-fold axis\" (which is a pentamer of ~5×735 aa≈ 3700 residues). Good PROXY for large multimer runtime.\n\nAlso \"Alphafold2 Multimer eventually generates a pentameric structure but it is not the correct 5-fold axis\" — supports baseline failure mode.\n\nLet me now search for: ColabFold runtime GPU minutes quote; GROMACS ns/day A100 benchmark; LightDock; LRP6 AAV; PDB IDs for AAV capsid structures (6IH9 AAV2-AAVR, 1LP3 AAV2, AAV9 structures). And maybe \"AAV9 Ly6a receptor\" (PHP.eB uses Ly6a — Hordeaux et al. 2022? Actually \"AAV-PHP.eB binds LY6A\" — Huang et al. 2022? \"AAVR-independent...\"?). Search: \"Ly6a AAV-PHP.eB receptor\".\n\nAlso \"LRP6 AAV\" — maybe there's an AAV paper on LRP6? Not that I know. Might be Wnt-related BBB receptor. If nothing, I'll treat LRP6 as DESIGN (part of the receptor panel per the option) and note no public AAV–LRP6 binding readout → it's a negative/unknown in the panel.\n\nAlso the \"plate- and bead-based\" assay format: Capsida deck quotes cover it. For our emitted calls format, we'll emit per-capsid×receptor rows with bind/no-bind + score + geometry, formatted as a plate map (rows=capsids, columns=receptors) and bead-coded panel — DESIGN.\n\nLet me also verify the Capsida PDF is fetchable and grab exact quotes. The search result gave highlights from the PDF; I need exact sentences — the highlights look like extracted text. I'll fetch the PDF to confirm exact quotes. webfetch may handle PDFs? It says it can read PDFs (Read tool can read PDFs; webfetch converts to markdown). Let me try webfetch on the Capsida PDF and the Lir blog and the biorxiv preprint.\n\nAlso Lambda pricing page: https://lambda.ai/pricing — cite with given page_text.\n\nLet me do the next batch of searches/fetches."
